<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<TestCaseEntity>
   <description>Built from live findings confirmed by Danniecia 9/24/2026. New UI enforces different max-length values than Old UI for Company Name (200 vs. 100) and City (100 vs. 50) — not a gap, just a mismatch, worth confirming with product which is intended. First Name, Last Name, and Email have NO max-length enforcement on New UI at all, unlike Old UI which caps all three at 50 (see TC_Registration_FieldMaxLength_OldUI) — this is a real old-vs-new gap, candidate bug. Job Title also has no cap, but Old UI's Job Title has no documented cap either, so that's an observation, not a regression. Assertions check the field's actual resulting value length after typing an oversized string, rather than assuming a specific enforcement mechanism (HTML maxlength attribute vs. JS truncation) — this avoids the same wrong assumption that made the original postal-code finding go stale. Company Name/City assertions went through three attempts before landing on the real mechanism (9/29, confirmed via screenshot): setText (round 1) and sendKeys (round 2) both correctly returned an untruncated 220-char value; round 3 added a Tab blur, still 220. All three were "failing" for the same reason — the assumption that the field truncates was simply wrong. The field does NOT truncate at all: it accepts the full oversized value and shows a validation error instead ("Company name must be 200 characters or fewer"), the same mechanism as Postal Code. Final assertions check for that error text rather than a truncated length. City's exact error wording ("City must be 100 characters or fewer") was a guess extrapolated from Company Name's pattern — confirmed correct on the 9/29 run. All 27 steps passing.</description>
   <name>TC_Registration_FieldMaxLength</name>
   <tag></tag>
   <comment></comment>
   <recordOption>OTHER</recordOption>
   <testCaseGuid>e856a247-98c8-4d80-ae1b-83e81e9aadc1</testCaseGuid>
</TestCaseEntity>
